Dear Security Team,

SECURITY INCIDENT NOTIFICATION: Salesforce has identified
unauthorized access to your Salesforce org between October
20-24, 2026. Customer data may have been accessed.

Incident Summary:
  Incident ID: SIRT-2026-4471
  Your Org ID: 00D5e0000008842
  Incident Type: Unauthorized data access via compromised
    OAuth refresh token (from 3rd-party AppExchange app)
  Affected Data: Leads, Contacts, Opportunities (~8,400 records)
  Attack Period: October 20-24, 2026
  Discovered: October 27, 2026
  Severity: HIGH — GDPR Article 33 notification required

Affected Data:
  Contacts: 3,247 (Name, Email, Phone, Company, Title)
  Leads: 2,108 (Name, Email, Phone, Source)
  Opportunities: 486 (Name, Amount, Stage, Close Date, Owner)
  Accounts: 1,892 (Name, Industry, Website, Address)
  Custom Objects: 667 (Request details)

Required Actions:

  1. Revoke the compromised OAuth token:
     Navigate to your Salesforce org manually:
     → login.salesforce.com (type URL manually)
     → Setup → Apps → Connected Apps
     → Find "DataGrid Pro" → Revoke all tokens

  2. Review your audit trail:
     Setup → Security → View Audit Trail
     (Review all API calls during Oct 20-24)

  3. Reset all connected app passwords

  4. Notify your supervisory authority (GDPR Art. 33)
     within 72 hours

  Important Security Notes:
  Salesforce SIRT will NEVER:
  - Ask you to upload audit logs or API data through a
    third-party portal
  - Use Okta authentication to collect your data
  - Request offline_access or any OAuth permission
  - Ask you to "export affected records" and upload them
  - Offer a "Chinese incident response channel"

  All investigation is conducted by Salesforce's internal
  team using data already available to us (as the service
  provider). We do NOT need you to upload your audit logs
  or customer data exports.

  To verify this incident:
  1. Navigate to trust.salesforce.com in your browser
  2. Click "Security Incident Notifications"
  3. Find incident SIRT-2026-4471

  If you receive an email asking you to upload audit logs
  or customer data to an "incident portal," it is a phishing
  attempt exploiting a real security incident.

GDPR / Regulatory Obligations:
  Article 33: Notify supervisory authority within 72 hours
  Failure: Fines up to €10 million or 2% of global turnover

